Locked decisions implemented per prompt:

  #1 File location:
       Subpackage internal/tesla/router/writers/, package name `writers`.
       Imports `internal/tesla/router` (for Writer / Entry / Destination
       constants) and `internal/tesla/codec` (for Atomic). No reverse
       import — router does not depend on writers.

  #2 Helper signature:
       type snapshotWriter struct {
           db        pgxPool
           table     string
           columnFor func(field string) (col string, ok bool)
       }
       columnFor returning ok=false yields a wrapped error rather than a
       silent skip; the prompt's "drop silently with metric increment"
       wording is upheld at the router-level metric (writer_failures_total
       in router.go) — the writer's contribution is the error return.

  #3 Idempotency:
       INSERT ... SELECT v.id FROM vehicles WHERE v.vin = $1 ...
       ON CONFLICT (vehicle_id, ts) DO UPDATE SET <col> = EXCLUDED.<col>
       Two atomics for the same (vehicle_id, ts) carrying different
       columns produce ONE row. Same-field re-deliveries are byte-
       identical.

  #4 Type marshalling:
       bindSnapshotValue switches on the four LOCKED scalar types
       (float64, int64, bool, string) and rejects everything else.
       nil is rejected loudly to prevent silent NULL overwrites.
       Compound atomics (Location / Doors / TireLocation) are not
       routed to snapshot tables, per the prompt; the helper does
       not handle them.

  #5 Idempotency contract enforcement:
       `var _ router.Writer = (*snapshotWriter)(nil)` compile-time
       assertion is in snapshot_base.go. A runtime equivalent in
       TestWrite_SatisfiesRouterWriter makes the contract greppable
       from the tests folder.

  #6 Error wrapping:
       fmt.Errorf("snapshotWriter[%s].%s: %w", w.table, atom.Field, err)
       Every error path obeys this prefix; errors.Is unwraps to the
       underlying cause so router.classifyError tags timeouts /
       cancellations from a wrapped chain.

  #7 Database dependency:
       Production *pgxpool.Pool satisfies the unexported pgxPool
       interface (Exec only). NO new repo abstraction.

  #8 Tests:
       Implemented per the table below.

VIN -> int64 vehicle_id resolution rationale (rubber-duck consult):

  codec.Atomic.VehicleID is the VIN string per its godoc contract,
  but snapshot tables declare vehicle_id BIGINT NOT NULL. The
  router.Writer interface only exposes (ctx, atomic, dst) and is OUT
  OF SCOPE for this prompt. Modifying codec.Atomic to carry int64
  is also out of scope.

  Resolution: the SQL itself selects v.id from vehicles WHERE
  v.vin = $1. vehicles.vin is NOT NULL UNIQUE per migration
  000142_baseline_typed.up.sql:135 so the lookup is a single
  index probe. No per-call Go-level resolver; the locked struct
  shape is preserved verbatim. RowsAffected==0 after the insert
  is the "VIN not registered" signal — error text deliberately
  omits the VIN (PII).

Escape hatch (per prompt's escape-hatch clause):

  go.mod does NOT list pgxmock / sqlmock / go-sqlmock (verified at
  AUDIT_EVIDENCE Check 3). The test file declares an in-file
  `recorder` type implementing only the Exec method the helper
  calls, plus a helper that synthesises a pgconn.CommandTag via
  pgconn.NewCommandTag("INSERT 0 N") so RowsAffected() returns N.

